
  Next fork, and it's the one that decides whether this actually solves "debug production issues": [1mwhere do browser logs
[0m  [1mgo?[0m Node's answer is easy (structured JSON to stdout, platform collects it). The browser has no equivalent — a
  [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m on a user's laptop is invisible to you unless something ships it.

  - [1mShip to an endpoint you own[0m — the core batches records and POSTs them to a collect URL. No new dependencies, fits
    the zero-dep repo, and you own the data. The catch: [1mthere is no backend in this repo.[0m [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m points at
    [38;5;153mhttps://api.example.com/login[39m, a stub. So this option is only real if a collector endpoint exists or someone will
    build one — otherwise I'd be writing a transport that posts into the void.
  - [1mThird-party service (Sentry or similar)[0m — you get grouping, stack traces, release tracking, and a UI on day one,
    which is most of what "debug production issues" actually means in practice. Cost: your first runtime dependency, an
    SDK you don't control, and log data leaving your infrastructure.
  - [1mConsole only, structured[0m — same record shape, no shipping. Honest scaffolding, but it does not let you debug
    anything you can't reproduce locally. I'd only pick this as a deliberate phase one.

  Hard to reverse: the record schema, once a collector or Sentry is parsing it. Easy to reverse: the sink itself — the
  whole point of the shared core is that swapping the transport touches one file.

  My recommendation is [1mship to your own endpoint[0m, designed so the sink is pluggable, [3mprovided[0m a collector exists. If
  nothing will receive the POSTs, I'd rather build console-only now with the transport seam in place than ship dead
  code.
